            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of the Wright Brothers</strong><br>\nThe Wright brothers, Orville and Wilbur, were American inventors and pioneers of aviation. Born in Dayton, Ohio, Orville on August 19, 1871, and Wilbur on April 16, 1867, they developed an interest in flying from a young age. Their curiosity was sparked by a toy helicopter their father brought home, which was based on an invention by French aeronautical pioneer Alphonse Pénaud.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Beginnings of Their Experiments</strong><br>\nBy the 1890s, the Wright brothers started their own bicycle repair shop and factory, which funded their growing interest in flight. They used the profits to pursue their aeronautical experiments, conducting extensive research on how to create a controllable flying machine. They believed that control of the aircraft was the most crucial aspect of flying.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Glider Experiments</strong><br>\nFrom 1900 to 1902, the Wright brothers conducted glider experiments in Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, because of its strong winds and soft sandy surfaces, ideal for safe landing. They built and tested a series of piloted gliders and developed their skills in controlling these machines in the air, focusing on three axes of flight: pitch, roll, and yaw.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Invention of the Three-Axis Control</strong><br>\nThe brothers' key contribution to aviation was inventing three-axis control, which allowed the pilot to steer the aircraft effectively and to maintain its equilibrium. This concept was crucial for fixed-wing aircraft and is still used in all kinds of airplanes today, marking the beginning of modern aeronautical engineering.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The First Powered Flight</strong><br>\nOn December 17, 1903, the Wright brothers achieved the first successful powered flight near Kitty Hawk. Orville piloted the gasoline-powered Wright Flyer, flying for 12 seconds and covering 120 feet. Although their achievement went largely unnoticed at the time, this event marked a monumental milestone in human history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Demonstrations and Recognition</strong><br>\nIt was not until 1908, after many improvements to their designs, that the Wright brothers started to gain widespread fame. They conducted public demonstrations in the United States and Europe, finally receiving the recognition they deserved. Their flights convinced the public and governments that aeronautics had practical applications.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
